  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "KDxVlF9kUW6QHsSfAk2BFUzLncw=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:27.948Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Abilian ships its own open-source products \u2014 Abilian SBE, a CRM, and Nua, a self-hosted PaaS \u2014 built on Python and Django. Nua's concern is deployment: getting self-hosted applications running under the operator's own control.\n\nThe research's finding is that none of these products carries a business-event record. Self-hosting answers where an application runs and who administers it; the collaboration and CRM products hold current state about people and content. Missing across all three is the attributed record of what was decided within them, which is a different kind of object from either configuration or content.\n\nBecause these are Abilian's own products on a stack the firm authored, the gap is addressable as a product decision rather than as a client integration \u2014 the same pattern that appears at the other product-owning leads in the corpus. Python and Django also mean the dispatch points are ordinary framework extension points rather than a new runtime to introduce.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 next**\n- Abilian (FR Paris) \u2014 St\u00e9fane Fermigier, directeur de la publication \u00b7 contact@abilian.com \u00b7 https://abilian.com\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Ships its own OSS products (Abilian SBE, CRM, Nua self-hosted PaaS) on Python/Django. Nua deploys self-hosted apps; none of them carry a business-event record.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:28:27.948223Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:27.948228Z"
    }
  },
